    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "The full suite is green \u2014 **42 tests, 0 failures, 0 errors**, BUILD SUCCESS.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented an optional, region-validated `postcode` on the create-owner endpoint. The change spans the API contract, entity, persistence, and validation pipeline:\n\n1. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 Added an optional `postcode` property to the `OwnerFields` schema (4-digit string). Because `Owner` composes `OwnerFields` via `allOf`, the generated `OwnerFieldsDto` (request) and `OwnerDto` (response) both gain the field, and MapStruct maps it by name in both directions with no mapper edits.\n\n2. **`Owner.java`** \u2014 Added the `postcode` field (`@Column`) with getter/setter.\n\n3. **Schema DDL** (hsqldb, h2, mysql, postgres) \u2014 Added a `postcode` column to the `owners` table. Tests run on the `hsqldb` profile.\n\n4. **`ValidateOwnerFields.java`** \u2014 Added `validatePostcode`, running in the validating first step (so failures become a 400 with `postcode` in the `errors` array). The rule:\n   - **Absent/blank** \u2192 skipped (optional; backward-compatible).\n   - **Present** \u2192 must be exactly 4 digits, else rejected.\n   - For a city with a **known region** (Sydney\u2192NSW 2000\u20132099, Melbourne\u2192VIC 3000\u20133099, Brisbane\u2192QLD 4000\u20134099), the value must fall in range, else rejected.\n   - A city with **no known region** accepts any 4-digit postcode.\n\n5. **`ApplyOwner.java`** \u2014 Carries `postcode` through the `PUT /owners/{ownerId}` update path for consistency.\n\nThe three Cp29 cases (stores valid postcode, accepts owner without postcode, rejects malformed) and Cp13 (namesakes with differing postcodes) all pass, and no existing checkpoint regressed.",
